**Corollary 104.3 (the all-beta family).** Let
$U_1=\langle\beta,\langle\beta,\beta\rangle\rangle$ and
$U_{j+1}=\langle\langle\beta,\beta\rangle,U_j\rangle$.
For every $k\ge1$, $U_k$ is in $\mathcal D$, has $k$ disjoint beta
cherries and $b=2k+1$ beta leaves. More generally any earned all-beta
tree with $b\ge3$ and $k$ such cherries is admitted. In this family

$$
a=0,\ c=b,\ N=3b-1,\ I=2b-1,
\quad M=7b-1,\quad s=3b.
\tag{104.4}
$$

The general-preparation vector is
$(b,b-1,0,2b^2,2b-1,1)$; the marker vector is
$(b,b-1,0,2k,1,1)$. Thus the all-beta source also has its own general
preparation, independently of its special marker run.

**Proof of the corollaries.** The recursive displayed terms are finite
birth-legal ordered trees. Each step in $V_j$ adds a disjoint beta
cherry and keeps the alpha at a right-ending address; each step after
$U_1$ adds a disjoint beta cherry and there is no alpha. Counting leaves,
branches and beta markers gives (104.3)--(104.4) from $N=2b+c-1$.
Substitution into (103.2), (104.1) and (104.2) proves all the stated
sizes and preparation counts. These are distinct specimens and runs,
not interchangeable coordinates of a single cheaper specimen. $\square$

